# The Canteen - a cook, a standing order, and a plate nobody comes for.
# Written for the library. One person, one procedure, one decision: whether to keep a habit that outlived its reason.

title: The Canteen
start: start

== start ==
bg dining
music room_tone
The canteen serves eleven to two, and at two it is a room with chairs in it. You
have worked it for nineteen years: the rota on the wall, the till that does not
connect to anything, the urn, and the board with the day's two things on it.
Wednesday is pie and it has been pie for nineteen years, which is not a rule
anybody wrote down. It is just what Wednesday is.
-> the_board

== the_board ==
The board says PIE. Under it, on a strip of card you wrote yourself a long time
ago, in the same marker, it says:
for R. no onion.
You have made a no-onion pie every Wednesday since the card went up. R sits at
the end table under the clock, or sat, and eats it in eleven minutes, or ate,
and the plate goes back on the stack at the end of the shift either way.
You have not seen R since the summer.
* Make it, same as always. -> make_it
* Ask somebody about the card. -> ask_around
* Take the card down. -> take_it_down

== make_it ==
You make it, because it is Wednesday, and the pie is the pie. Onion in the big
one, none in the small one, and the small one goes in the low oven so it stays
warm past two.
You take it out at half past two and put it on the end table under the clock
with a paper napkin and a fork, and you clear it away at three.
* Ask somebody about the card. -> ask_around
* Leave the plate out at the end of the shift, just once. -> leave_it
* Write a note for yourself and get on with the washing up. -> wash_up

== ask_around ==
You ask Doreen, who does the sandwiches and has been here longer than you.
doreen: R? That's the inspector's lad. The one that came with him in the summer
on the survey. Used to get here at ten past two when the queue was gone.
you: And now?
doreen: Survey finished in August, love.
She goes back to the bread, and then, over her shoulder, the way she says
things that matter: doreen: He ate it every week for a year, though.
* Take the card down. -> take_it_down
* Put the card away in the drawer instead of the bin. -> the_drawer
* Carry on. It is Wednesday. -> make_it

== take_it_down ==
You peel the tape off the board and the card comes away with a strip of paint
on it, and where it was is a rectangle that is darker than the rest of the
board. You put it in the bin under the counter, on top of the peelings.
At two there is a room with chairs in it and no card, and Wednesday is pie,
and the small one is not in the low oven, and the shift is twenty minutes
shorter than it has been every Wednesday for years.
-> ending_stopped

== the_drawer ==
You put the card in the drawer with the spare markers and the rota from 2019
and the key to the store that nobody has used since the store was rekeyed.
The dark rectangle on the board fills in, over a month, the way those things
do, and R comes up at ten past two in a queue that is not there, and you make
the small pie anyway because you know how.
-> ending_kept

== wash_up ==
You write *R - no onion* on the back of a delivery note and put it in the
drawer, which is what you do with things you are not dealing with, and you get
on with the washing up.
The urn ticks. Doreen's radio plays the same four songs. At two the room has
chairs in it.
-> ending_pattern

== leave_it ==
You put the small pie on the end table with a napkin and a fork and you do not
clear it away at three. You stack the chairs around it and mop the far half of
the floor and lock the till and it is still there when you go.
In the morning it is not there, and the plate is on the stack, washed, which
is not how you left it, and nothing about that is your business, so you fill
the urn.
-> ending_left

== ending_stopped ==
The Wednesday after that you make one pie and it sells out by one o'clock, and
the Wednesday after that you make two, and by the end of the month Wednesday
is just a day with pie on it.
You are not sad about it. It is a board in a canteen and you are a cook, and
nineteen years of a small pie is a long time for a thing that was never on the
rota, and you have done the sensible thing, and you know you have.
You keep the card, though. It is in the bin under the peelings and you take it
out again at the end of the shift and put it in the drawer with the markers,
and you do not tell anybody, and that is the whole of it.
-> END

== ending_kept ==
You make the small pie every Wednesday for the rest of the year, and the year
after that, and the till stays broken and the urn ticks and the card in the
drawer gets a fold in it and stays legible.
It is not for R by then. R is a lad who ate pie in a queue that is not there,
and you are a woman who makes it, and the two of you agree about a Wednesday
without either of you saying so.
You have worked here nineteen years. Most of what you know is in your hands.
-> END

== ending_pattern ==
*R - no onion* goes in the drawer and the drawer fills up and in nineteen years
you have not once looked in it for anything but a marker.
The canteen closes in the spring, not because of anything, because the site is
moving. On the last Wednesday you make the small one out of habit and it goes
in the low oven and nobody asks for it, and you eat it yourself at the end
table under the clock in eleven minutes, which is exactly how long it takes.
-> END

== ending_left ==
The plate is washed and on the stack and nothing about that is your business.
You think about it in the car and in the morning and when you are filling the
urn, and by Thursday you have decided to ask Doreen about the survey and the
inspector and the lad, and Doreen says what Doreen says, which is that the site
is moving in the spring and the lad was kind and eighteen and got here at ten
past two.
You make the small pie on the last Wednesday too. You put it on the end table
with a napkin and a fork, and you clear it away at three, and nineteen years is
a long time and it was not wasted.
-> END
